"## Grouping page: The Indian Ocean\n\nA wind that reverses twice a year let printers in Gujarat, potters in Zhejiang and builders at Kilwa work for buyers they never met.\n\n**Card**\n\n| Part | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Kind | Network: a set of sea routes, ports and merchant communities, not a state or a people (NET005, the umbrella row; sub-circuits NET006 Arabian Sea, NET007 Bay of Bengal, NET008 South China Sea) |\n| Defined by (who, when) | Named from outside and after the fact. The *Periplus of the Erythraean Sea* (mid-1st century CE, a Greek-Egyptian merchant; TXT073) described its western half port by port. Historians made \"the Indian Ocean world\" a unit of study in the late 20th century; the canon row names K. N. Chaudhuri and Abdul Sheriff (not re-checked here). The sailors, merchants and makers inside it used their own names for seas and routes |\n| Span | c. 2500 BCE (Harappan–Gulf contact, canon) to the present. This walk runs from the 1st century CE to 2011, with most steps between c. 800 and 1500 CE |\n",
"| Places | The East African coast from Shanga to Kilwa; the Red Sea and Aden; the Gulf (Siraf, Hormuz); Gujarat and the Malabar coast (Calicut, Mangalore); the Straits (Malacca); the south China coast (Quanzhou) |\n| Membership confidence | Documented for ports, excavated finds and letters. Probable for most routes: a sherd found at Kilwa shows where a bowl ended, not the ships it took. Contested for who carried the trade and who profited |\n| Leaves out | The inland miners, hunters and farmers who supplied gold, ivory and grain; the sailors and caulkers; the printers and dyers, almost never named; women, who appear mostly through men's letters; the people who were enslaved and forcibly moved along the same routes, whom the old sources list among cargo |\n",
"| Rights line | Objects: Met Open Access (Public Domain where `isPublicDomain` is true) and Cleveland Open Access (CC0) textiles and ceramics may be shown. Geniza manuscripts: link out to the holder; the Princeton Geniza Project page text is CC BY 2.0, images are not. Belitung cargo: link out only (commercial salvage). British Museum Kilwa and Swahili material: link out only (non-commercial). Lenj: link out to UNESCO; per-photo licences. No image of enslaved people is shown without a content note and its source's framing |\n\n**Walk**\n\n| # | Step title | What the learner sees and reads | Nodes | Claim and confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | The wind that turns | Sailors timed their year to a wind that blows one way, then the other. Over the Arabian Sea the southwest monsoon runs from about June to September; the northeast monsoon blows in winter. The *Periplus* credits a pilot, Hippalus, with laying a course \"straight across the ocean\" (§57). | NET005, NET006, TXT073 | Wind timing: documented. That Hippalus found the route: contested (see What is argued) |\n",
"| 2 | A ship sewn together | Shipwrights stitched this hull plank to plank with cord, without nails. It sank off Belitung, Indonesia, in about the 830s. Michael Flecker found \"every timber was fastened with stitching\", in African *Afzelia* planks with teak through-beams from India. | PLC248, TEC143, OBT295, MAT095 | Construction and timbers: documented (Flecker). That it was built in the western Indian Ocean: probable |\n| 3 | Seven ports, seven towns | Ports were towns where people settled, married and prayed, not only stops. Siraf served the Abbasid Gulf; excavations from 1966 found a \"massive haul\" of Chinese and Islamic pottery. Aden, Calicut, Hormuz and Malacca each held one sea gate; Quanzhou's Qingjing Mosque and a statue of Mani show a mixed merchant town. | PLC277, PLC294, PLC221, PLC278, PLC245, PLC190, PLC076 | Documented (Whitehouse's excavations; UNESCO 2021 for Quanzhou). Dates for Aden, Calicut, Hormuz and Malacca are the canon's, approx. |\n",
"| 4 | Cloth printed for Cairo | Printers and dyers in Gujarat stamped this cotton for buyers in Egypt. The fragments were dug from rubbish mounds near Fustat in the 1920s and 1930s. Radiocarbon dates in the Ashmolean's Newberry collection run from about 1010 CE (±55) to the late 1600s. | NET055, MAT056, PLC003; Met 30.112.40; Met 30.112.28; Cleveland 1951.516 | Made in Gujarat, found at Fustat: documented (museum attributions; Barnes). Workshops and names of makers: not known |\n| 5 | Bowls set into walls | Potters in China made glazed bowls and dishes that reached the Swahili coast. Twenty sherds of 9th-century Changsha ware came from Shanga; Longquan greenware and Jingdezhen blue-and-white followed. From the 13th century, Swahili builders set such bowls into the façades of pillar and dome tombs. | OBT070, TEC034, NET009, NET054, NET012, NET011, PLC076, CIV004; Cleveland 1964.164 | Finds and dates: documented (Carfantan; UNESCO Kilwa). What the bowls meant to their owners: we read them as display and, from oral tradition, protection (interpretive). Tombs are described, not shown |\n",
"| 6 | A merchant's letters | Abraham Ben Yiju, born in Ifriqiya about 1100, was trading from Mangalore by 1132. He ran a bronze workshop and sent pepper, cardamom and brass bowls to his partner Madmun in Aden. On 17 October 1132 he freed an enslaved woman, Ashu; she took the name Berakha. | PER326, TXT067, DOC-001, DIA004, TXT066, PLC294 | Letters, trade and the deed: documented (Goitein; Princeton Geniza Project). That Ashu became his wife: probable (most specialists). Her own voice: not recorded |\n| 7 | Families across the water | Merchant families lived on both shores for generations. Gujarati traders ran cloth, beads and credit and settled in Malacca, Mombasa and Zanzibar; Tomé Pires recorded them in Malacca c. 1512–15. By the mid-1930s the Hadrami diaspora numbered about 110,000, a third of all Hadramis. | DIA006, DIA005, COM061, PLC245 | Documented. The 1930s figure: documented as cited (Wikipedia after Engseng Ho, 2006) |\n",
"| 8 | People moved by force | *Content note: enslavement.* Enslavers moved people across this ocean from at least the 1st century CE into the 1800s. The *Periplus* lists enslaved people beside cloth and grain (§13, §31, §36); we name them as people. In lower Iraq, enslaved workers cleared salt-crusted land and rose in revolt (869–883); Zanzibar's slave market closed in 1873. | COM203, PLC080, POL011, TXT073, NET005 | Documented. Who the rebels of 869–883 were: contested. Numbers moved across the ocean: not known with any precision |\n| 9 | A lenj on the slipway | Shipwrights on Iran's Gulf coast still build wooden lenj by hand. UNESCO inscribed the skills of building and sailing them in 2011, on its Urgent Safeguarding list. Navigators read the sun, moon and stars and named each wind; in 2011 the practitioners were few and mostly older. | PRA143 (project PR-011), TEC144, OBT295; PRA023 (Swahili jahazi building) | Documented, dated to UNESCO's 2011 file. Present number of yards: not known to us |\n\n",
"**Members.** This walk lights 10 networks and diasporas, 11 places, 3 people, 5 texts, 5 techniques and object types, 3 materials, 2 communities, 2 practices and 4 open objects. Networks and diasporas: the Indian Ocean world (NET005), the Arabian Sea circuit (NET006), the Nanhai route (NET009), the Kilwa gold route (NET011), Swahili dhow trade (NET012), the porcelain trade (NET054), the Indian cotton textile trade (NET055), Geniza merchants (DIA004), the Hadrami diaspora (DIA005) and Gujarati merchant communities (DIA006). Places: Siraf, Aden, Calicut, Hormuz, Malacca, Quanzhou, Kilwa Kisiwani, Fustat, Zanzibar Stone Town, the Belitung wreck and, through the letters, Mangalore (no canon row). People: Abraham Ben Yiju (PER326); Ashu, named in his deed but with no row of her own; Ibn Battuta (PER325), who visited Kilwa in 1331. Texts: the *Periplus* (TXT073), the Geniza documents (TXT066), Ben Yiju's letters and accounts (TXT067, project DOC-001), the Kilwa Chronicle (TXT048) and the *Rihla* (TXT072). Techniques and types: sewn-plank boat building (TEC143), dhow building (TEC144), celadon (TEC034), the dhow (OBT295) and the celadon bowl (OBT070). Materials: cotton (MAT056), teak (MAT",
"095), coir (MAT069, for stitching; not confirmed for Belitung). Communities: Waswahili (COM061) and Siddi (COM203). Practices: lenj building and sailing (PRA143) and Swahili dhow building (PRA023). The Swahili coast (CIV004) is a civilisation label that opens onto these rows; it is not a member ranked above them.\n\n**Neighbours.** Overland, the Silk Roads (NET001) carried some of the same silk and ceramics by relay. The Red Sea network (NET032) and medieval Mediterranean trade (NET023) joined it at Aden and Fustat; the Geniza merchants worked in both. At its eastern end it meets the Nanhai route (NET009) and the Chinese maritime diaspora (DIA007). From 1498 the Cape Route and the East India companies (NET033) cut into it by force. Vasco da Gama reached Calicut that year (OCC053); Portuguese forces sacked Kilwa and Mombasa in 1505 (OCC054). Parallels elsewhere: trans-Saharan trade (NET010), which also moved gold, cloth and cowries (NET013); the transatlantic trafficking of enslaved Africans (NET035), which was larger in scale and shorter in span; Austronesian voyaging (NET045), which reached Madagascar across the same ocean.\n\n",
"**Afterlives.** The word \"calico\" is named after Calicut (canon PLC221). Kilwa Kisiwani and Songo Mnara were inscribed as World Heritage in 1981; Quanzhou followed in 2021 as \"Emporium of the World in Song-Yuan China\". The Belitung cargo was sold to Singapore's Sentosa Leisure Group in 2005 for US$32 million; the Smithsonian cancelled its exhibition in 2011. Siraf is now under the village of Taheri and near oil and gas works (Current World Archaeology, undated web page). The Siddi descend from East Africans who came as soldiers, sailors, merchants and enslaved people. The 2011 census counted 10,477 in Karnataka and 8,661 in Gujarat. In 2011 UNESCO recorded that fibreglass boats were replacing wooden lenj and that yards were turning to repair work. \"Maritime Silk Road\" is the name many governments and tourist boards now use for these routes (canon other names).\n\n**What is argued.**\n\n",
"- *Who found the monsoon route?* The *Periplus* (§57) credits the Greek pilot Hippalus with learning to cross open water. Other readings hold that Arabian Sea sailors trading between Africa, India and Southeast Asia knew the pattern long before (Wikipedia, \"Monsoon of South Asia\"). We show the Greek text as one outsider's claim.\n- *Whose coast?* Colonial-era writers framed the Swahili towns as \"Arab\" or \"Persian\" colonies. African archaeology since the 1960s reads them as African towns working in Indian Ocean trade (canon CIV004, naming Chittick, Horton and Chami). The Chinese bowls in tomb façades support the second reading: local builders chose where they went (Carfantan).\n- *What was the revolt of 869–883?* One reading: a rising of enslaved East Africans, \"Zanj\", against Abbasid landowners. Ghada Talhami and M. A. Shaban argue it was a wider uprising of the poor, with free Africans, Bedouin and peasants (Wikipedia, \"Zanj Rebellion\"). Both are shown.\n",
"- *Should a salvaged cargo be shown?* The Smithsonian judged that showing the Belitung finds would condone \"commercial exploitation of unscientifically excavated objects\". Singapore and Indonesia read the cancellation as a slight (Trafficking Culture). The Atlas links out and states the salvage history.\n\n**Apply** (F1.13 Networks; links to F1.6, F1.9 and F1.10)\n\n- **Maker (M).** The Met's 14th-century Gujarati print (30.112.28) copies woven silk patterns from Iran and Egypt in stamped, mordant-dyed cotton. Design up to three blocks that give a costly surface through a cheaper process, for a buyer in another country. Note what your process can and cannot copy, and write a maker's credit for the printer.\n- **Briefer (B).** Write a one-page order from Madmun in Aden to a workshop in Mangalore for bronze bowls. Set the deadline by the sailing season, not a calendar date. Name who carries the risk if the ship is lost. Name the workshop's makers, even as \"the casters of Ben Yiju's workshop\", and state what you do not know about them.\n\n**Candidate open objects**\n\n| Holder and ID | What it is | Date range | Licence as stated | 1970 provenance status | Record |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| The Met, 30.112.40 | Textile fragment with chevron pattern; made in India, Gujarat; found near Fustat, Egypt | 13th–14th century | Public Domain (`isPublicDomain: true`) | Purchased 1930 (V. Everit Macy Gift); provenance \"Found near Fustat, Egypt\": pre-1970 | [Met 448534](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/448534) |\n| The Met, 30.112.28 | Textile with arabesque and palmette design; block-printed, mordant-dyed cotton; made in Gujarat, found near Fustat | 14th century | Public Domain (`isPublicDomain: true`) | Purchased 1930: pre-1970 | [Met 448522](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/448522) |\n| Cleveland Museum of Art, 1951.516 | Fragment with decorated bands; cotton, stamped resist and dyed; India, Gujarat | probably 1400s | CC0 | \"found at Fostat in Egypt; [Group F, Pfister … (Paris, 1938)]; (Adolph Loewi)\"; purchased 1951: pre-1970 | [clevelandart.org/art/1951.516](https://clevelandart.org/art/1951.516) |\n",
"| Cleveland Museum of Art, 1929.845 | Fragment, block printing on cotton; Egypt, Mamluk sultanate. Local printers using the Indian method, with fish motifs | 1200s–1300s | CC0 | Paul Mallon, Paris, sold to the museum 1929: pre-1970 | [clevelandart.org/art/1929.845](https://clevelandart.org/art/1929.845) |\n| Cleveland Museum of Art, 1964.164 | Dish with two fish in relief, Longquan ware, Zhejiang. Stands for the type; this dish has no East African findspot | mid-1200s | CC0 | Record gives no acquisition history; accessioned 1964 (gift of Severance A. Millikin) | [clevelandart.org/art/1964.164](https://clevelandart.org/art/1964.164) |\n| Princeton Geniza Project, record of IOM D 55.10 (held by the Institute of Oriental Manuscripts, Russian Academy of Sciences) | Deed of manumission for Ashu, written by Ben Yiju in Mangalore. *Content note: enslavement* | 17 October 1132 | Page text CC BY 2.0; no image on the page; manuscript image rights sit with the holder | Not applicable (archive document); holder's acquisition history not stated | [geniza.princeton.edu/documents/5501](https://geniza.princeton.edu/documents/5501/) |\n",
"| The Met, 2002.448 | Oval bowl, Changsha ware, the type carried on the Belitung ship | 8th–9th century | Public Domain (`isPublicDomain: true`) | Gift 2002; no earlier history published. Hold until provenance is checked, given the Belitung salvage market | [Met 65101](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/65101) |\n\nNo open-licensed object with an East African findspot was found in the Met, Cleveland or AIC collections. For step 5 we propose the Cleveland dish as a type, a drawing of our own of a tomb façade with inset bowls (from published plans, not photographs of graves), and a link out to excavation reports. For step 9 we propose a link out to UNESCO's lenj page, or our own photographs, taken with a yard's consent.\n\n**Sources**\n\n- [Made in India, Found in Egypt: Red Sea Textile Trade in the Thirteenth and Fourteenth Centuries (Met, Ayesha U. Shaikh, 2023)](https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/red-sea-textile-trade)\n- [Indian Block-Printed Textiles in Egypt: The Newberry Collection (Ashmolean, Ruth Barnes)](https://jameelcentre.ashmolean.org/collection/7/10236/10319)\n",
"- [Met object 30.112.40](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/448534) and [API record](https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/448534)\n- [Met object 30.112.28](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/448522)\n- [Met object 2002.448](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/65101)\n- [Cleveland Open Access API, 1951.516](https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/1951.516)\n- [Cleveland Open Access API, 1929.845](https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/1929.845)\n- [Cleveland Open Access API, 1964.164](https://openaccess-api.clevelandart.org/api/artworks/1964.164)\n- [Ruins of Kilwa Kisiwani and Ruins of Songo Mnara (UNESCO World Heritage)](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/144)\n- [Quanzhou: Emporium of the World in Song-Yuan China (UNESCO World Heritage)](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1561)\n- [Traditional skills of building and sailing Iranian Lenj boats in the Persian Gulf (UNESCO ICH)](https://ich.unesco.org/en/USL/traditional-skills-of-building-and-sailing-iranian-lenj-boats-in-the-persian-gulf-00534)\n",
"- [Construction of Iranian Lenj boats (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Construction_of_Iranian_Lenj_boats)\n- [A Ninth-Century Arab Shipwreck in Indonesia (Michael Flecker, Smithsonian)](https://asia.si.edu/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/shipwrecked-07-flecker.pdf)\n- [Belitung Shipwreck (Trafficking Culture)](https://traffickingculture.org/encyclopedia/case-studies/biletung-shipwreck/)\n- [Chinese Porcelain and Ceramics from the East African Coast (Caroline Carfantan, SEACS)](https://www.seaceramic.org.sg/wp-content/uploads/East_African_Coast-1.pdf)\n- [Siraf, Iran (Current World Archaeology)](https://www.world-archaeology.com/travel/siraf-iran/)\n- [Periplus of the Erythraean Sea (ToposText, Schoff translation)](https://topostext.org/work/491)\n- [Monsoon of South Asia (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monsoon_of_South_Asia)\n- [Legal document: IOM D 55.10 (Princeton Geniza Project)](https://geniza.princeton.edu/documents/5501/)\n- [Abraham Ben Yiju (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Ben_Yiju)\n- [Hadhrami diaspora (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadhrami_diaspora)\n- [Tomé Pires (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom%C3%A9_Pires)\n",
"- [Zanj Rebellion (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zanj_Rebellion)\n- [Siddi (Wikipedia)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siddi)\n- [F1 Groundwork (Claude Docs), grouping pages for Mingei, Nok and the Igun Eronmwon guild](https://claude.ai/code/artifact/6d02bdf9-1689-4a2b-b1fe-258e11817b83)\n"
