"## Grouping page: Indus Valley civilisation\n\nBrick drains, chert weights and carved seals, about 2600–1900 BCE: a modern name gathers them, and nobody knows what their makers called themselves.\n\n**Card**\n\n| Part | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Kind | Civilisation, as commonly named (CIV048). It is a search label that opens onto the Harappan archaeological cultures beneath it. It is not a self-name, and the Atlas does not rank it against other civilisations. |\n| Defined by (who, when) | John Marshall and the Archaeological Survey of India, announced in the *Illustrated London News* in September 1924. \"Harappan\" comes from the type site, Harappa. Archaeologists and museums in Pakistan, India and elsewhere use both names. \"Sindhu–Sarasvati civilisation\" is used mainly by supporters of Indigenous Aryanism and is politically contested. Jim Shaffer (1992) recast the whole as an \"Indus Tradition\" of eras (PRD142). |\n| Span | About 3300–1300 BCE (canon, approximate). Beneath it: Early Harappan about 3300–2600 BCE (ARC138), Mature Harappan about 2600–1900 BCE (ARC139), Late Harappan about 1900–1300 BCE (ARC140). Earlier farming villages such as Mehrgarh (ARC137) sit before it, not inside it. |\n",
"| Places | The Indus and Ghaggar–Hakra plains, with Gujarat and the Balochistan edges; today Pakistan and northwest India. Cities: Harappa (PLC215), Mohenjo-daro, Dholavira, Rakhigarhi, Lothal (PLC216); craft town Chanhu-daro. By 2002 over 1,000 Mature Harappan settlements had been reported, just under a hundred excavated. |\n| Membership confidence | Documented for finds excavated at named sites. Probable for museum seals labelled only \"Indus Valley\", which are attributed by style. Contested at the edges: hinterland villages, and whether Late Harappan communities still belong. |\n| Leaves out | Its makers' own name and language, since the signs are unread. Whether the signs are writing at all. The farmers, herders and hill communities of Balochistan and the hinterland. Its neighbours in Dilmun, Magan, the Helmand and the Oxus. Present-day people of Sindh, Punjab, Gujarat and Haryana. The Late phases, often told as \"collapse\". |\n",
"| Rights line | Met and Cleveland records listed below are CC0 or public domain and may be shown. One CC0 Commons photograph shows weights in the National Museum, New Delhi. Harappa.com images are © Harappa.com: link out only. Objects in Pakistan's and India's national collections: link out unless the holder licenses. Burials at Harappa and Rakhigarhi, and all human remains, are never shown. |\n\n**Walk**\n\n| # | Step title | What the learner sees and reads | Nodes | Claim and confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | A drain in a Mohenjo-daro street | Walk a Mohenjo-daro street and a covered brick drain runs beside you. Builders laid streets at right angles, with baked-brick houses, wells and soak pits for sewage. No palace or royal tomb has been identified there. | CIV048; ARC139; TEC155 (fired brick); Mohenjo-daro (no canon ID; proposed PLC) | Planning, drains and soak pits: documented (UNESCO). No identified palace: documented as an absence, not proof of equality. |\n",
"| 2 | Bricks under a railway | Harappa's mounds were known, and quarried for bricks, long before archaeologists came. Charles Masson described Harappa after travelling in Punjab from 1829. In the mid-1850s, Harappan bricks were laid under about 160 km of Multan–Lahore railway track. Daya Ram Sahni dug there from about 1921; Marshall announced the finds in 1924. | PLC215; CIV048 | Documented. We name the brick-taking plainly: the site was damaged for colonial railway building. |\n| 3 | Reservoirs at Dholavira, mounds at Rakhigarhi | At Dholavira, in Kachchh, builders cut sixteen or more reservoirs into stone. UNESCO dates occupation to about 3000–1500 BCE and records bead, shell and metal workshops. Rakhigarhi's eleven mounds are put at about 350 hectares; critics say the figure joins separate settlements. | Dholavira, Rakhigarhi (no canon IDs; proposed PLC); ARC138–ARC140 | Reservoirs and workshops: documented. Rakhigarhi as \"the largest\" site: contested. Cities differ: Dholavira builds in stone, Mohenjo-daro in brick. |\n",
"| 4 | A chert cube on a balance pan | A small chert cube sits on a balance pan. Weights at Harappa double from 0.856 g in a 1:2:4:8:16:32 series; the commonest is about 13.7 g. Above that the steps become decimal, and the same system turns up across the settlements. | OBT165; INV173; PRD142; Commons photo (National Museum, New Delhi) | System and values: documented (Harappa.com captions). Use for trade and perhaps taxes: probable, not recorded. |\n| 5 | A seal cutter's one-horned animal | A seal cutter carved a one-horned animal into a square of steatite. The finished seal was fired: the Met describes Met 49.40.1 as \"burnt steatite\". Seals were probably pressed into clay to mark goods or property. | OBT126; MAT009; Met 49.40.1; Met 49.40.2; Cleveland 1964.104 | Making: documented. Use: probable (\"likely employed to identify merchandise or property\", Cleveland). What the animal and the object before it meant: interpretive; the Met reads the object as possibly an altar or incense burner. |\n",
"| 6 | Five signs nobody can read | Above the animal runs a short line of signs nobody can yet read. About 5,000 inscriptions are known, averaging about five signs, with 400 to about 700 distinct signs estimated. At Dholavira ten signs, each about 37 cm high, were set on a board by the north gateway. | INV006 (links to F1.5); Met 49.40.4 (signs only); Dholavira | Counts: documented as estimates. Is it writing? Contested: Farmer, Sproat and Witzel (2004) argued the signs are non-linguistic emblems; Rao and colleagues (2009) argued their statistics sit closer to languages. |\n| 7 | A bead driller at Chanhu-daro | At Chanhu-daro in 1935–36, excavators found beads at every stage of making. Makers struck long slips of stone, flaked and ground them, then bored them with stone drills. Kenoyer links these hard-stone drills to the long, slender carnelian beads; shell workers sawed conch into bangles. | MAT006 (carnelian); MAT082 (conch shell); TEC043 (faience); Chanhu-daro (no canon ID; proposed PLC); PLC216 (Lothal bead workshop) | Stages and drills: documented (Mackay's report). Who organised the work, households or a central authority: argued (see below). |\n",
"| 8 | A carnelian bead at Nippur | A carnelian bead catalogued as Indus work was excavated at Nippur, Iraq, in 1957–58. Akkadian texts name Meluhha, and most specialists place it in the Indus region. Sargon's inscription says ships from Meluhha, Magan and Dilmun tied up at the quay of Agade. | NET016; CIV060; CIV061; PLC289; PLC265; Met 59.41.42 | Bead and text: documented. Meluhha as the Indus region: probable (scholarly consensus, not a self-name). Lothal's basin as a dock for this trade: contested. |\n| 9 | A painted bowl from the hills | Potters at Damb Sadaat, in Balochistan, made this bowl outside the cities' reach. The Met reads its shape and painting as showing contact with the Indus cities in the 3rd millennium BCE. After about 1900 BCE the large cities shrank and settlement moved east; the causes are argued. | Met 57.99.2; ARC137; PLC214; CIV070; CIV089; ARC140; PRD093 | Bowl and contact: documented. After 1900 BCE: we do not tell a \"rise and fall\". Shrinking cities are documented; the cause is contested. |\n\n",
"**Members.** In the canon: 1 civilisation-name (CIV048); 3 archaeological cultures (ARC138 Early, ARC139 Mature, ARC140 Late Harappan); 1 period scheme (PRD142, Shaffer's eras); 2 places (PLC215 Harappa, PLC216 Lothal), with 4 more proposed (Mohenjo-daro, Dholavira, Rakhigarhi, Chanhu-daro); 2 object types (OBT126 stamp seal, OBT165 Indus weights); 3 \"earliest known\" rows (INV006 Indus script, INV173 standard weights, INV099 wild silk); 4 materials (MAT006 carnelian, MAT009 steatite, MAT082 conch shell, MAT022 faience); 2 techniques (TEC155 fired brick, TEC043 faience). Eight verified objects: four Met seals, one Cleveland seal, the Nippur bead, the Damb Sadaat bowl and a photographed set of weights. The key members are the seals, the weights and the carnelian beads, because they travel between every city.\n\n",
"**Neighbours.** Before it: the farming village of Mehrgarh (ARC137, PLC214). Around it: hill communities of Balochistan (Damb Sadaat), the Helmand craft town of Shahr-i Sokhta (CIV070, PLC273), and the Oxus towns (CIV089, ARC104). Across the sea, through NET016: Dilmun (CIV060, POL099, PLC289), Magan and the Umm an-Nar communities (CIV061, ARC061, PLC292), and Mesopotamia, where Indus carnelian reached Ur (PLC265, PER279). After it: Late Harappan regional crafts (ARC140) and the Vedic period (PRD143), whose link to the Indus heritage is argued and politically charged. Parallel names elsewhere, with no ranking: Dilmun, Magan, Helmand and Oxus are also \"civilisations as commonly named\" in the canon.\n\n",
"**Afterlives.** Bead-making in Khambhat (Cambay), Gujarat, still works agate and carnelian. Kenoyer, Vidale and Bhan recorded it in 1989 and 1990: heating nodules, chipping, sawing, grinding, diamond-tipped drilling and polishing. Merchant families ran the workshops; Bhil labourers mined the stone; women strung the beads. They used this to argue that standard products need not mean state control. \"Agates of Cambay\" was registered as an Indian Geographical Indication in 2008 (Culture and Heritage, 2023). That page also reports silicosis among workers and dates the Muslim bead trade to Baba Ghor, about 1500 CE. In 2023 ThePrint profiled Anwar Husain Shaikh, a fifth-generation bead-maker who hand-drills Harappan-style beads. Its claim that today's makers descend from Harappan craftsmen is not evidenced; the continuity shown is of stone and technique. Remembered: Mohenjo-daro became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1980, Dholavira in 2021. Sold: museum shops and brands reuse the \"unicorn\" seal; Cleveland's own wall text still calls the sites \"mysterious\", which this page does not repeat.\n\n**What is argued.**\n",
"- Are the signs writing? Farmer, Sproat and Witzel (2004) argue they are non-linguistic emblems: texts average 4.6 signs, the longest single surface has 17, and frequent signs rarely repeat within a text. Rao and colleagues (2009) argue the signs' conditional entropy is closer to natural languages than to non-linguistic systems. Both sides still publish; no decipherment is accepted.\n- What happened after about 1900 BCE? Giosan and colleagues (2012) link shrinking and eastward-moving settlement to river and monsoon change, and use the word \"collapse\". Petrie (2019) stresses the cities' long resilience, notes that \"different social, economic and political structures\" were in place by about 1900 BCE, and calls the causal data inconclusive. Shaffer's scheme (PRD142) names it \"Localisation\", not collapse.\n- Was Lothal's basin a dock? S. R. Rao, its excavator, argued a dockyard. Leshnik (1968) and Yule (1982) argued a water tank, since the inlet seems too narrow for sea-going boats. Later finds of foraminifera and salt crystals suggest seawater once filled it.\n",
"- Who ran the cities? No palaces or royal tombs are identified. Petrie argues several elite groups of roughly equal standing, with ways of limiting concentrated power. Kenoyer, Vidale and Bhan's Khambhat study warns that standard beads and weights can come from many workshops, not only from a state.\n\n**Apply.** F1.5 (where the canon routes INV006 and INV173), with the seals and the weights.\n- Maker (M): write the label for Met 49.40.1 in three tiers, each true on its own. Show the signs but do not translate them; say who made the seal, what is known, and what is argued.\n- Briefer (B): write the brief clause for a brand borrowing Indus motifs. Ban \"lost civilisation\" and invented translations; require the holder, licence and 1970 status of any seal image; name Pakistan and India as the source countries.\n\n**Candidate open objects**\n\n| # | Holder | Accession / ID | Object | Date range (holder's words) | Licence (as holder states) | 1970 provenance status | Record URL |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| 1 | The Met | 49.40.1 | Stamp seal and modern impression: unicorn and incense burner (?); burnt steatite | ca. 2600–1900 BCE (Mature Harappan) | Public Domain (Open Access; `isPublicDomain: true`) | Passes: \"1902–1934, acquired by John H. Marshall from the Montgomery district of the Punjab Province of British India\"; bought from Marshall 1949. Note: Marshall headed the survey that dug Harappa, which sits in that district. | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324062 |\n| 2 | The Met | 49.40.2 | Stamp seal: buffalo with incense burner (?); steatite, burnt, glazed | ca. 2600–1900 BCE | Public Domain (Open Access) | Passes: same Marshall history, acquired 1949 | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324063 |\n| 3 | The Met | 49.40.3 | Stamp seal fragment: unicorn and incense-burner (?), inscription | ca. 2600–1900 BCE | Public Domain (Open Access) | Passes: same Marshall history, acquired 1949 | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324064 |\n",
"| 4 | The Met | 49.40.4 | Bar-shaped rectangular plaque seal (signs only); steatite | 3rd millennium BCE | Public Domain (Open Access) | Passes: same Marshall history, acquired 1949 | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324065 |\n| 5 | Cleveland Museum of Art | 1964.104 | Seal with Unicorn and Inscription; \"possibly kaolin\" | c. 2000 BCE | CC0 | Passes: from the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, by exchange (? – 1964). Earlier history not stated; the MFA co-sponsored the 1935–36 Chanhu-daro dig, but the record does not name a findspot. | https://clevelandart.org/art/1964.104 |\n| 6 | The Met | 59.41.42 | Bead, carnelian; culture Indus, findspot Nippur | ca. 2900–2350 BCE | Public Domain | Passes: excavated 1957–58 by the Joint Expedition to Nippur; acquired 1958 through the Met's funding of the dig | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324872 |\n",
"| 7 | The Met | 57.99.2 | Bowl with painted decoration, Damb Sadaat (Balochistan) | ca. early to mid-3rd millennium BCE (Early Harappan) | Public Domain (`isPublicDomain: true`) | Passes: excavated 1950–51 by the American Museum of Natural History's Afghan Expedition; exchanged to the Met 1957 | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324704 |\n| 8 | Wikimedia Commons (photo by Gary Todd, 2015); objects in the National Museum, New Delhi | File:Harappan (Indus Valley) Balance & Weights.jpg | Balance and cubical weights (photograph) | Not dated on the file; canon dates the type to Mature Harappan, about 2600–1900 BCE | CC0 1.0 (photograph) | Not stated; objects are in India's national collection, no accession number given | https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harappan_(Indus_Valley)_Balance_&_Weights.jpg |\n\n",
"Checked and not used: Cleveland 1973.160 and 1973.161 (CC0 seals) are first recorded with the dealer William H. Wolff and sold to Cleveland in 1973, so they fail the 1970 test. Met 1978.58, 1986.280 and 1999.22 (animal figures) were acquired after 1970; their provenance was not checked. No CC0 record of an Indus chert weight was found at the Met or Cleveland; use item 8, a drawing of our own, or link out to Harappa.com. The Met's text calls the script \"only partially deciphered\"; this page says undeciphered, following Rao and colleagues (2009).\n\n**Sources**\n- [Met, Stamp seal 49.40.1](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324062)\n- [Met, Stamp seal 49.40.2](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324063)\n- [Met, Stamp seal fragment 49.40.3](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324064)\n- [Met, Bar-shaped plaque seal 49.40.4](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324065)\n- [Met, Bead 59.41.42 (Nippur)](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324872)\n- [Met, Bowl 57.99.2 (Damb Sadaat)](https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/324704)\n",
